  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species is found in coastal waters of the North Pacific Ocean. It extends from Japan in the west, along Russia and the Bering Sea, the Gulf of Alaska and south to Baja California, Mexico (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2014). Type locality: North-western Pacific Ocean, Japan, Hokkaido Island (Jereb&#160;<em>et al</em>. 2014).",
    "population": "There is no population information for this species.",
    "habitats": "This species&#160;can be found in a range of habitats including sand and mud, but it has a preference for rocky reefs or boulder areas with sand-shell substrate where it inhabits dens. This species has a variance in depth depending on where it is found, north or south of its range, with northern members inhabiting intertidal reefs and southern members living at depths of over 1,500 m. This species is a nocturnal hunter and has a wide range of prey, including both vertebrates and invertebrates. Its main source of food is bivalve and gastropod molluscs and decapod crustaceans, which it consumes by drilling through the shell. It also consumes brachiopods, echinoderms, assorted fishes, shark eggs and seabirds. This species has a number of predators which include sharks, sea lions, seals, large fishes and land mammals such as mink. Mating occurs in summer in deep offshore water. In autumn and winter, as many as 100,000 eggs are then spawned inshore. Eggs are laid throughout the year in strings in rock crevices. Development time is temperature-dependent and can take around 5 to 6 months, with planktonic hatchlings then spending from 1 to 2 months in the plankton. Individuals have been found to feed from gill nets around Japan.&#160;Although this species is shy, on occasion it has been recorded to show aggressive behaviour towards divers, even biting (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2014).",
    "threats": "Overfishing is a potential threat to this species.",
    "measures": "There are currently no conservation measures in place for this species.      Further research is needed to assess the population size, trends and threats      that may be affecting this species, including from potential fishing pressures.",
    "use_trade": "This species is an important commercial resource and pot and trap harvesting occurs on a large scale in the northeast Pacific and off Japan. This species is mainly caught through dive harvesting off British Columbia, with large animals exported to aquariums (Jereb <em>et al.</em> 2014).",
    "rationale": "<em>Enteroctopus conispadiceus </em>has been assessed as Least      Concern because it has a wide geographic range. Even though it is heavily harvested, the scale of this is  unknown and  females lay such large numbers of eggs that the population  is likely to be constantly  replaced. We  recommend  that further research is     conducted to  determine its  population size, trends and threats, such as from harvesting.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
